<dialog id="sources"><button class="icon close" aria-label="Close scientific context" title="Close"><i data-lucide="x"></i></button><span class="eyebrow">EVIDENCE & INTERPRETATION</span><h2>A model, not a recovered film.</h2><p>The sequence follows the evolution figure on the first page of Nakamura et al. The paper proposes a cold, ice-bearing parent, internal heating and aqueous alteration, followed by disruption and reaccretion.</p><dl><dt>Supported sequence</dt><dd>Accretion of rock and ice; ice melting; water-rock reactions; cooling; a later impact; preservation and reaccretion of less-heated material.</dd><dt>Artist's interpretation</dt><dd>Disk structure, collision paths, light, material colors and internal textures. Early collisions are authored examples, not outputs of the paper's thermal model. Not every real collision produces growth.</dd><dt>Light & color</dt><dd>The external disk view represents scattered light. The cold midplane and interiors use illustrative lighting to make normally obscured material visible. Process colors are categorical overlays, not visible emission or a computed temperature field.</dd><dt>Scale & time</dt><dd>Chapters change scale and compress very different timescales. No measured orbital positions, snow-line radii or mineral proportions are implied.</dd><dt>Orgueil</dt><dd>Orgueil's parent is unknown. This Ryugu-based model is a comparison context, not an identification of Orgueil's parent body.</dd></dl><p class="source-links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn8671" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nakamura et al. / Science</a><a href="https://global.jaxa.jp/press/2022/09/20220923-1_e.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JAXA / Stone-team results</a><a href="https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1333/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ESO / Shielded disk midplanes</a></p></dialog>
<script>import '../scripts/origins-study/main.js';</script></body></html>
